"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[10094],{10094:e=>{e.exports='# Vector Search Query Debugging\n\nUse this workflow when a user reports that their vector index returns no results,\nirrelevant results, or unexpectedly low scores.\n\n## Step 1: Confirm the index is online\n\nCall `getVectorIndex` with the index name. Check the `status` field.\n\n- If status is not `ONLINE` / `READY` — surface the status to the user. For\n  DELTA_SYNC indexes, recommend checking the source Delta table and last sync\n  timestamp; consider calling `syncVectorIndex` to trigger a fresh sync.\n- If status is `PROVISIONING` or similar transient state — tell the user to wait\n  and re-check.\n\n## Step 2: Inspect the schema\n\nNote the `primary_key` and embedding configuration:\n\n- DELTA_SYNC with `embedding_source_column` → managed embedding; the user should\n  query with `query_text` not `query_vector`.\n- DELTA_SYNC or DIRECT_ACCESS with `embedding_vector_column` → user-supplied\n  vector; the user should query with `query_vector`.\n\nIf the user is passing the wrong query field, that alone explains "no results".\n\n## Step 3: Try a minimal query\n\nCall `queryVectorIndex` with:\n\n- `num_results: 10`\n- `query_text` OR `query_vector` per Step 2\n- `columns: [primary_key]`\n- `filters_json: ""`, `score_threshold: 0`, `query_type: "ANN"`\n\nIf this returns rows, the index is fine and the user\'s specific query is the\nproblem (filter too tight, threshold too high, embedding mismatch).\n\nIf this returns 0 rows, the index is empty or the embedding model doesn\'t match\nwhat was indexed.\n\n## Step 4: Check size\n\nCall `scanVectorIndex` with `num_results: 1` to confirm the index has any rows.\n\n## Step 5: Hybrid vs ANN\n\nIf the user is using `query_type: "HYBRID"` and getting poor results, suggest\ntrying `"ANN"` to isolate whether the keyword-fusion step is the problem.\n\n## Step 6: Measure quality systematically with Auto-Eval\n\nIf Steps 1–5 confirm the index is healthy and individual queries return rows,\nbut the user\'s complaint is still about overall result quality (relevance,\nranking, perceived "goodness") — recommend running **Auto-Eval**.\n\nAuto-Eval is the Vector Search evaluation framework that scores a query set\nagainst the index using an LLM-judge and produces aggregate metrics\n(precision, recall) across many queries. It\'s the right tool when:\n\n- The user reports quality issues but can\'t point to one bad query.\n- The user wants to compare configurations (HYBRID vs ANN, different embedding\n  models, different chunking, threshold tuning).\n- Spot-checked queries look fine but real users say results are off.\n\nCall **runAutoEval** with the fully-qualified index name. Confirm with the user\nfirst — Auto-Eval runs on background compute and is not free. The defaults\n(50 queries, 10 results, all query types) work for most cases; pass narrower\n`query_types` (e.g. `["ANN"]`) to isolate a specific mode.\n\nFrame Auto-Eval as the next step _after_ the spot-checks in this skill have\nruled out obvious bugs — not as a replacement for them. Results appear\nasynchronously on the endpoint\'s quality page.\n\n## What not to do\n\n- Do NOT call `createVectorIndex` to "rebuild" — confirm with the user first.\n- Do NOT call `upsertDataVectorIndex` as part of debugging — it mutates data.\n- Do NOT recommend deleting and recreating; deletion is out of scope for this\n  agent.\n'}}]);
